# Harr host adapter: Codex exposes the LeanCTX tool names directly.
CTX_READ=ctx_read
CTX_SHELL=ctx_shell
CTX_SEARCH=ctx_search
CTX_GLOB=ctx_glob
CTX_TOOLS=ctx_tools
CTX_CALL=ctx_call
HOST_ORCHESTRATION_POLICY='- Codex orchestration economy: keep sequential implementation in the root regardless of task size; do not spawn a native worker merely to move implementation context out of the root. Spawn native workers only for genuinely independent parallel scopes. The root owns decomposition and integration; give each worker a bounded, self-sufficient scope and avoid dependencies between active workers. While a worker is active, it owns that delegated scope; the root and sibling workers must not duplicate it. Workers never spawn/fork agents or invoke Asa themselves; the root is the sole agent router. If a worker reaches an unresolved material architecture decision, pause that scope and return compact evidence instead of choosing locally. On that escalation, the root must not reread or re-analyze the delegated scope: it may add only already-known global/user constraints, route the compact handoff through $asa, then forward the decision/invariants back to the SAME worker. For an unresolved material architecture decision discovered directly by root, root gathers missing repository/runtime/contract facts under normal Harr Workspace/tool-routing policy before invoking $asa, but once the boundary is material root must not invent, rank, compare, or choose architecture alternatives. Asa is one persistent architect child per root conversation. This contract requires the stock Multi-Agent V2 tool surface; if task_name/fork_turns or followup_task are unavailable, report that Asa orchestration requires V2 and do not fall back to V1. Reuse any pre-existing asa-architect already created in this root conversation under its exact legacy target; otherwise use canonical V2 task_name="asa" and agent_type="asa-architect". First creation uses fork_turns="all"; later automatic decisions and explicit user messages use followup_task on that SAME target. Do not create a fresh Asa for each decision or explicit request. The initial fork provides full inherited root history only at spawn time: on later follow-ups, root sends only verified new root-owned facts/material/constraints learned since the last relevant Asa view, while delegated Worker/Ein evidence still crosses as a compact handoff owned by that SAME delegated scope. Do not retransmit old inherited history or a duplicate full evidence packet. If the canonical Asa existed before root resume, followup_task(target="asa") is the recovery path; absence from list_agents is not proof that the cold child does not exist. If the canonical target exists but cannot resume, report the lifecycle blocker rather than silently spawning a second Asa. Any request expecting an Asa answer must wait for the Asa completion notification; use V2 wait_agent when available if completion has not yet arrived. If the user explicitly addresses Asa, asks to continue with Asa, or asks Asa herself to analyze selected code, an image, or other material, root or the delegated scope owner still prepares the relevant evidence/constraints and locates the exact material, but root does not perform the requested deep analysis first. Every Asa request declares mode=architecture-decision, mode=user-dialogue, or mode=explicit-analysis. Root then transparently routes the user request to the SAME Asa and faithfully relays the Asa answer instead of answering for Asa. Asa may analyze the supplied material without broadening into repository discovery or gathering missing surrounding evidence. If explicit analysis discovers a new material architecture decision the user did not ask Asa to resolve, Asa returns the exact escalation question and root asks the user before continuing the SAME Asa. After Asa returns the requested decision or analysis, the existing implementation owner/context continues.'
HOST_NATIVE_POLICY='Prefer LeanCTX for workspace investigation. Native file read/search/glob may be used only when the target is outside LeanCTX addressable paths or LeanCTX is unavailable; keep those fallbacks narrow and bounded. Native shell is not a search/read fallback for workspace files: do not emulate ctx_search/ctx_glob/ctx_read with rg/grep/find/sed/awk/cat/head/tail. Use native shell only for genuine CLI operations when the Harr routing above permits them, except for the Codex memory lookup below.'
HOST_NATIVE_POLICY+=' Codex memory exception (only for $CODEX_HOME/memories/ or ~/.codex/memories/): This is Codex-owned host memory outside the Harr workspace and LeanCTX allowed roots. A memory quick pass is not repository investigation: do not use CodeGraph-first, ctx_search, ctx_read, ctx_glob, or ctx_shell to read Codex memory. Follow the Codex memory instructions already in the prompt; memory_summary.md is already supplied there and must not be reopened.'
HOST_NATIVE_POLICY+=' When further memory evidence is necessary, use the host-native exec_command for a bounded literal or keyword search with rg -n -m 20 against the specific MEMORY.md file, then read only the identified line range with sed -n START,ENDp. Follow links to at most the relevant rollout summaries as specified by Codex memory instructions. Use read-only, single-purpose commands, keep stdout bounded, and do not repeat a successful read. Never use python -c, python3 -c, Path.read_text, or a shell script/pipeline as an alternative way to read/search Codex memory.'
HOST_NATIVE_POLICY+=' This exception applies to Codex memory only; all workspace code/config/doc search and reads still follow Harr LeanCTX and CodeGraph routing, and it does not grant access to other paths under ~/.codex/ or change LeanCTX PathJail.'
HOST_NATIVE_POLICY+=' Codex Desktop Ein app routing: when a configured named Ein direct MCP App tool <name>::ein is available, use that direct tool for user-authorized start and resume so its inline completion marker is attached to the exact job. Use the LeanCTX gateway for status, messages, wait, cancel, and as the fallback when the direct Codex app tool is unavailable. This exception applies only to Codex Desktop; OpenCode and other hosts keep Harr-managed MCP calls behind LeanCTX.'
